Ilmari Karonen wrote:
 > Geoduck <geoduck42@gmail.com> wrote:

 >> I suspect other people will have similar
 >> complaints, so I have added quick n' dirty "blown
 >> up" 30x18 and 40x24 versions, which should both
 >> still be cleaner than a 30x15 setup.

 > Just for fun, I ran your 20x12 tileset through the
 > scale2x algorithm
 > (<http://scale2x.sourceforge.net>).

 > The result is here:

 >   http://vyznev.net/misc/geoduck40x24scale2x.png

 > It's not perfect, but it's a decent start at
 > least.  Note that ideally each tile should be
 > scaled separately, with appropriate edge handling;

Anybody have a Script-Fu for GIMP that takes apart
an image as gridded, and stacks the extracted tiles
as layers in a single image instead? That would make
rescaling a tile set almost painless. The sheer
tedium of treating all those tiles individually
kills my enthusiasm really quickly.

 > I just ran the whole picture through the algorithm
 > at once, but I did a quick visual comparison and
 > didn't notice any significant bleeding.

That does an astonishingly good job, especially
about not bleeding even when a tile's contents go
"right to the edge" of another tile.

The only places I'd do hand cleanups would be the
places where plain pixel replication would have
been the better thing to do: the '+' characters in
the spellbooks lose their sharpness to the point
that it is an issue, primarily because they are so
small at the start. The same artifacts in the
families of letters with square corners, the F, E,
H, L, T, D, and B, at least, and the gelatinous cube
'b' interior, are completely endurable without
fixups.

xanthian; my digital lettersets used to be graded by
my user community with a 40 power field lens, a
frequently used tool always at reach on the desk of
each of them, so I got quickly taught to be _very_
picky about lettersets I made, or suffer their
cartographers' wrath. The habit endures these 29
years later, it seems.

Now if only there were an algorithm with that kind
of performance for non-integer scaling, since I
still want a 15x30 tile set that stays inside my
1280x800 screen boundaries as full a dungeon level.
I used to have a similar such algorithm, written by
me, based on Bresenham's (sp?) line interpolation
algorithm, once upon a time, but it stayed with my
employer, long since gone bankrupt.


